  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 AM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nRadar imagery shows light rainfall moving through the area in \nconjunction with a Clipper lifting from the north shore of Lake \nSuperior towards James Bay. Radar imagery is also showing drier air \nworking its way over the west early this morning, with the synoptic \nrainfall ceasing from west to east mainly before dawn this morning. \nCAA working into the region behind the synoptic precipitation is \nlooking to bring gusty northwest winds to the Keweenaw and along \nLake Superior east of Marquette today as the cooler but drier air \ngenerally keeps the area under mostly sunny skies save for maybe \nsome scattered lake enhanced/effect clouds over the east. While the \nclear skies will allow for uninhibited solar radiation to reach the \nsfc, with 850mb temperatures around 1 to 4C expect highs to be \nlimited to the mid 50s to lower 60s (warmest in the south central). \nWith a reinforcing shot of CAA coming in tonight, expect light lake \neffect rain/drizzle showers to pick up over the eastern U.P., \ncontinuing until Monday as high pressure ridging approaches and \neventually passes over the area late in the day; expect the rainfall \naccumulations over the eastern U.P. tonight through Monday to be on \nthe order of nothing more than a few hundreths of an inch. Expect \nfairly similar conditions on Monday as compared to today, save for \nthe sfc being a touch drier and temperatures maybe a degree or two \nmore moderated on the high and low ends during the daytime. \n\nAfter the high pressure ridging moves through late on Monday, \nanother Clipper low phases with a low pressure from northern Canada \nbefore digging towards the Upper Great Lakes on Tuesday. While there \nis still some disagreement on how far the phasing low will dig \nbefore lifting over northern Ontario Tuesday night, rain chances \nhave subsequently increased over the last 24 hours as guidance does \nnow suggest enough forcing to at least accomplish some light \nrainfall. That being said, with some dry air at around 850-700 mb \nneeding to be overcome, expect rainfall amounts to be rather light \nin the warm sector of the low, with current rain chances still \nlimited to around 10 to 25% with the highest chances in the eastern \nUP. As subtle CAA works into the area Tuesday night through \nWednesday, we could see a return of light lake effect rain/drizzle \nshowers over the northwest wind belts, mainly over the eastern Yoop. \nIn addition, expect gusty southerly winds in the warm sector on \nTuesday up to 30 to 35 mph at times by the afternoon hours, highest \nover the east half, and west to northwest winds of 25 to 30 mph over \nthe Keweenaw and along the Lake Superior shoreline east of Marquette \nagain on Wednesday. With rain chances increasing, the fire weather \nconcerns have dwindled in comparison to yesterday. Nevertheless, \nfire weather partners should keep an eye on the winds and RHs as the \nareas near Wisconsin could be drier than currently anticipated. \nWhile drier weather could be seen via sfc ridging overhead for the \nlatter portion of the work week, additional rainfall could be seen \nby next weekend as a potentially robust Clipper low forms and moves \nthrough the Northern Plains.",
